{% from "macros.njk" import callout, snippet with context %}

{% set design %}
<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-usage">Usage</h2>
  <p>Text fields collect free-form input. The default is the outlined style
     (1px border, no fill); <code>fl-field--filled</code> switches to the M2
     filled style (tinted fill, flat bottom indicator) for dense forms and
     search boxes. Both are plain HTML inputs — the CSS only styles them, so
     autofill, validation and mobile keyboards all behave natively.</p>
  <h3>Do use it when</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>You need <strong>single-line input</strong> (email, name, search) —
        wrap the input in <code>&lt;label class="fl-field"&gt;</code> with a
        <code>fl-field__label</code> span.</li>
    <li>You need <strong>multi-line input</strong> — a real
        <code>&lt;textarea class="fl-field__input"&gt;</code>; it auto-heights
        and resizes vertically only.</li>
    <li>The form needs <strong>inline guidance or validation</strong> —
        <code>fl-field__help</code> renders under the input and doubles as the
        error slot.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Don't use it when</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>One value out of a known list — that is a
        <a href="select.html">select</a> or
        <a href="selection-controls.html">checkboxes/radios</a>; a text field
        invites typos you then have to validate away.</li>
    <li>You need a custom dropdown menu — the custom listbox is planned for
        0.4 with the overlay infrastructure; until then the native control is
        the honest choice.</li>
    <li>The value is display-only — plain text, not a disabled field; disabled
        fields are for genuinely unavailable input.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

