    <ol>
      <li><strong>Label</strong> — 12px/500 above the input; it is a real
          <code>&lt;label&gt;</code> wrapping the control, so clicking it
          focuses the field.</li>
      <li><strong>Input</strong> — 44px tall, 1px <code>--fl-border</code>,
          4px radius; hover darkens the border, focus takes the accent border
          plus a flat 2px-looking inset ring.</li>
      <li><strong>Helper text</strong> — 12px under the input; on error it
          recolors to <code>--fl-error</code> and the border follows.</li>
      <li><strong>Filled variant</strong> — tinted <code>--fl-divider</code>
          fill, flat accent bottom indicator (2px on focus via inset
          box-shadow).</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-behavior">Behavior</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Structure is the API:</strong> <code>fl-field</code> is a
        <code>&lt;label&gt;</code> wrapping <code>fl-field__label</code>, the
        input, and optional <code>fl-field__help</code> — in that order. The
        association is native, no <code>for</code>/<code>id</code> wiring
        needed.</li>
    <li><strong>Two ways to mark an error:</strong> add
        <code>fl-field--error</code> on the label, or set
        <code>aria-invalid="true"</code> on the input — either alone works and
        they stack. The second exists so JS validation code never has to know
        the class name.</li>
    <li><strong>Helper doubles as the error slot</strong> — reuse the same
        element and swap its text; the color follows the error state
        automatically.</li>
    <li><strong>Filled variant</strong> (<code>fl-field--filled</code>) keeps
        the same label/help markup; only the box changes. Error state
        recolors its bottom indicator instead of the full border.</li>
    <li><strong>Textarea</strong> is <code>&lt;textarea
        class="fl-field__input"&gt;</code> — height auto, <code>rows</code>
        sets the initial size, resize is vertical-only so the layout can't
        blow out.</li>
    <li><strong>Disabled</strong> is the native attribute — dimmed text on a
        flat fill, hover darkening switched off. Dim the helper text by
        writing it in secondary color (the default) — the CSS does not style
        the label, so prefer <em>removing</em> optional guidance on disabled
        fields.</li>
    <li><strong>Custom dropdown is not here yet</strong> — a styled native
        <a href="select.html">select</a> ships in this release; the custom
        listbox menu is planned for 0.4 with the overlay work.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-accessibility">Accessibility</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The label wraps the input — real <code>&lt;label&gt;</code>
        association for free, including click-to-focus and screen-reader
        naming.</li>
    <li>Mark errors with <code>aria-invalid="true"</code> (which the CSS keys
        off) and keep the visible error text in <code>fl-field__help</code> —
        never color alone; the text is the announcement.</li>
    <li>Never remove the focus style — it is the only focus indicator the
        flat input has (accent border + inset ring).</li>
    <li><code>placeholder</code> is a hint, not a label — always ship a
        <code>fl-field__label</code>; placeholders disappear on input and are
        skipped by some screen readers.</li>
    <li>Disabled inputs are skipped by tab order — that is correct; if the
        field must stay discoverable, prefer <code>readonly</code> +
        explanation over <code>disabled</code>.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
{% endset %}

